WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.300
Leaving just enough open to interpretation
can make a movie immortal, ensuring that fans

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.840
will be left debating the meaning of their
favorites for years to come.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.760
In that vein, we've assembled an assortment
of movies that have left audiences puzzled.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.930
Here are a few of the most confusing plot
twists explained.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.019
"I missed out on the whole thing to be honest
with you.

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:19.600
I was confused by it.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.060
I was confused by the whole thing, if I'm
being honest."

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.859
Who's who in Lost Highway?

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:31.429
In 1997's Lost Highway, we meet Fred Madison,
a jazz musician accused of murdering his wife,

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:32.429
Renee.

00:00:32.429 --> 00:00:35.620
Fred goes to jail, but the next morning the
guards discover that Fred's place has been

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:39.170
taken by Pete Dayton, a car mechanic with
mild amnesia.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:43.410
Pete is soon entangled with Alice Wakefield,
a woman who looks identical to the murdered

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.410
Renee.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:48.390
The movie continually questions who's who,
all while a mysterious man follows them both,

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.089
videotaping the confusing carnage that ensues.

00:00:51.089 --> 00:00:55.899
The obsessive display of infidelity, characters
watching and being watched, and grisly murders

00:00:55.899 --> 00:01:00.420
might all seem familiar to anyone who watched
the O.J. Simpson trial in 1995.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.700
Nearly 10 years after Lost Highway's release,
director David Lynch would elaborate on the

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.930
connection between the movie and the Simpson
trial in his book Catching The Big Fish.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:09.930
Lynch wrote

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.590
"I wondered how, if a person did these deeds,
he could go on living, and we found this great

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:18.690
psychology term, psychogenic fugue, describing
an event where the mind tricks itself to escape

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:19.690
some horror."

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:24.340
Fred Madison is a man who murdered his wife
and invented an entire alternate life story

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.650
to escape his own guilt.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.080
It's fitting, then, that the movie ends with
a high-speed car chase in which Fred feels

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:33.340
out of control; at least it wasn't on the
405 highway like O.J.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.340
Vertigo's lookalikes

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:39.780
40 years before David Lynch confounded audiences
with Lost Highway's pair of lookalike characters

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.710
played by the same actress, Alfred Hitchcock
pulled a similar trick in Vertigo.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:48.370
The film introduces us to Scottie, a private
investigator who has retired from police work

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.980
due to his struggles with, well, vertigo.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:55.240
His old friend Gavin hires Scottie to trail
his wife, Madeline, because he fears she's

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.040
possessed by the spirit of her great-grandmother.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.420
Madeline is drawn to the mission where her
great-grandmother died, and rushes up the

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.480
bell tower.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.880
Scottie's vertigo prevents him from following,
and he watches in horror as she falls to her

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:07.880
death.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.039
He's so shaken that he's committed to an institution.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:14.020
Upon release, he meets a woman named Judy,
who looks eerily like Madeline.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:18.460
The two begin an unhealthy relationship as
Scotty remakes Judy to look like Madeline.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.860
"You're looking for the suit that she wore
for me.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.050
You want me to be dressed like her"

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:25.200
"Judy, I just want you to look nice."

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.510
A flashback reveals the twist: that the "Madeline"
Scottie knew was always Judy, who Gavin hired

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.180
in an elaborate plot to get rid of his wife.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.600
All of the supernatural elements of the story
were concocted to confuse Scottie, whose vertigo

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.590
was guaranteed to leave him a helpless witness
to the fake fatality.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.500
Parental revelations

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.850
After Rey was introduced in The Force Awakens
with vague questions about the identity of

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.010
her long-lost parents, The Last Jedi had her
confront the truth.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.180
In a shocking twist for viewers that were
expecting some variation of Luke Skywalker's

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.920
own twisted family free, it turns out that
her parents were nobody important.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.730
While some saw this twist as a cop-out, director
Rian Johnson had his reasons.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.959
Thematic parallels are an essential part of
the Star Wars formula.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.700
As George Lucas likes to say,

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.060
"Again, it's like poetry, they rhyme.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.620
Every stanza kinda rhymes with the last one."

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:18.680
On an episode of Empire's film podcast, Johnson
explained that he didn't just write that moment

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.490
to contrast the famous scene in Empire Strikes
Back.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:25.220
It's actually the most dramatic and difficult
thing for Rey to reckon with.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.010
Instead of being given a tailored role in
the story like so many heroes before her,

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.190
she gets told that her place isn't where she's
been looking for it.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.640
"Hardest thing to hear is 'nope, this is not
gonna define you.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:41.740
In fact, Kylo is going to use this to undercut
your confidence so that you'll feel you have

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.370
to lean on him for your identity."

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.849
It's now in her hands to find her own identity.

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:51.330
It's purposeful, and it's an intentional change
in the familiar patterns found in all previous

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.130
Star Wars films.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.239
Psycho killer

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:58.400
American Psycho is designed to leave audiences
wondering how much of what they've seen is

00:03:58.400 --> 00:03:59.540
actually real.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.970
As the film reaches its climax in an over-the-top
killing spree riddled with explosions and

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:08.980
swooping police helicopters, it becomes clear
that Patrick Bateman may not be an entirely

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.220
reliable narrator.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.540
The morning after the climactic chaos, Bateman
returns to the apartment where he'd been executing

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.949
people for weeks, ready to clean up the evidence
of his misdeeds.

00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:20.489
He's disturbed to find the place empty and
freshly painted.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:23.289
"You saw the ad in the Times?"

00:04:23.289 --> 00:04:27.340
"No… yeah, I mean, yeah.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:28.160
In the times."

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.360
"There was no ad in the Times."

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.580
What does this scene represent for the plot?

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.550
According to screenwriter Guinevere Turner,

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.139
"He did do all this stuff and he still cannot
stand out.

00:04:38.139 --> 00:04:39.139
[...] He's stuck."

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:42.949
Because Bateman's crimes are so unremarkable
in the film's viciously heightened universe

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:47.870
of Wall Street capitalism that all attention,
even punishment, will elude him forever.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.260
"Don't you know who I am?

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:52.260
I'm not Davis.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.700
I'm Patrick Bateman."

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.380
Twisted timelines

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:00.780
As the Fast &amp; Furious franchise has branched
out into ambitious new plot threads, it's

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.110
quickly revealed itself as a labyrinth of
non-linear narrative that must be explored,

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:06.919
in the words of the immortal Dominic Toretto,

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:09.460
"A quarter-mile at a time."

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:14.520
In the series' third film, 2006's Tokyo Drift,
newcomer Sean Boswell is taken under the wing

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.409
of Han, a racer who teaches him the power
of drifting.

00:05:17.409 --> 00:05:21.990
Unfortunately, Han is tragically killed during
a race, prompting Toretto to appear and declare

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.419
himself an old friend of Han's in the final
scene.

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:25.930
Now here's where the twist comes in.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:31.229
2009 saw the release of the series' fourth
film, Fast &amp; Furious, which reunited the original

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:32.229
cast and Han.

00:05:32.229 --> 00:05:36.370
Han says he's off to Tokyo, leading the audience
to believe that the fourth film takes place

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:37.770
concurrently with the third.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:42.389
But no, Han's back again in a more significant
role in Fast Five, and sticks around through

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:43.940
Fast &amp; Furious 6.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:47.659
The story finally catches up to Han's death
in a post-credits scene at the end of the

00:05:47.659 --> 00:05:51.569
sixth movie, which reveals that Deckard Shaw,
the villain of Furious Seven, was actually

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:54.139
responsible for the "accident" in Tokyo Drift.

00:05:54.139 --> 00:05:58.869
So, the series chronology means that Tokyo
Drift actually happens in between the sixth

00:05:58.869 --> 00:05:59.869
and the seventh movie.

00:05:59.869 --> 00:06:03.530
Although, don't expect the series to get any
simpler just because the timeline finally

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:04.550
matches up.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:08.879
Series writer Chris Morgan has recently speculated
that Han could come back yet again.

00:06:08.879 --> 00:06:09.879
And why not?

00:06:09.879 --> 00:06:11.150
After all, Han is family and

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:13.969
"you don't turn your back on family."

00:06:13.969 --> 00:06:15.659
Just a dream, right?

00:06:15.659 --> 00:06:19.479
Despite the popularity of the Nightmare on
Elm Street franchise, director Wes Craven

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:22.169
never envisioned the series living beyond
one film.

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:26.389
Executive producer Robert Shaye disagreed,
hoping to leave the door open for a follow-up,

00:06:26.389 --> 00:06:31.050
and the compromise they reached resulted in
a fairly baffling finale for the original

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.050
movie.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.419
Even though Nancy vanquished Freddy earlier
in the film, the ending scene seems to imply

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:39.680
his return, when she's trapped in a convertible
bearing Krueger's trademark red and green

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:40.680
stripes:

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:41.680
What does it all mean?

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.620
When Nancy reappears in A Nightmare on Elm
Street 3: Dream Warriors, she mentions that

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:47.520
Krueger got rid of her friends and her mother.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.960
We can assume, then, that Nancy undoing the
murders in her defeat of Krueger was a dream

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:52.960
in itself.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.249
In the documentary Never Sleep Again, Robert
Englund, the actor who plays Freddy Krueger,

00:06:57.249 --> 00:07:00.629
proposes his own theory that the film is a
precognitive nightmare:

00:07:00.629 --> 00:07:05.149
"Everything in it will happen, but it hasn't
really happened, she's just dreamt that it's

00:07:05.149 --> 00:07:07.710
going to happen, and she's trying to warn
everybody.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.240
And then it begins in the very ending."

